{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cg6VdDPzzxdcMSiPN8YVX", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "```\nTable 3 reports the estimates. Identity disclosure carries its own weight: the coefficient on the identity-disclosed indicator is 0.18 (SE = 0.04) in column 2, and it remains at 0.15 (SE = 0.05) in column 3 after we add product fixed effects. Disclosure also matters more for some reviewers than others. The interaction with reviewer tenure is 0.09 (SE = 0.03) in column 4, so the effect of disclosure grows with tenure. Throughout, star rating stays a strong predictor, with a coefficient of 0.42 (SE = 0.02) across all specifications, so identity effects appear alongside the rating effect rather than in place of it. Model fit improves as the specifications build, with R-squared rising from 0.31 to 0.47 across the columns. All coefficients are significant at the 1% level except the interaction, which is significant at the 5% level.\n```\n\nI kept every number, SE, and significance level as written, and the claims stay within what the table reports. The changes are structural. Each sentence now has a job (main effect, robustness to fixed effects, moderation, rating benchmark, fit), and the paragraph connects them back to your thesis that identity matters beyond the rating.\n\nOne call for you to check: \"the effect of disclosure grows with tenure\" is my reading of a positive interaction. If tenure is centered or coded differently in your models, adjust that clause to match."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1878, "cache_read_input_tokens": 1496, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1878}, "output_tokens": 7, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "